@page "/configuration"

<PageTitle>Configuration · Belemetry</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Configuration</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        Everything Belemetry does is set on one <code>BelemetryOptions</code>, passed to
        <code>AddBitBelemetryServices</code>. The tables on this page are reflected from the shipped assembly - names,
        types, the defaults a fresh options object really has, and the XML documentation you see in IntelliSense - so
        they cannot describe an option that does not exist.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> One registration</h3>
    <p>
        <code>AddBitBelemetryServices</code> follows the Options pattern and is idempotent: call it in every project
        that hosts Blazor, and every configure callback passed across calls runs, in order, on the same
        <code>BelemetryOptions</code>. A shared method called from both the server and the client project keeps the
        two in step, and each project adds what is its own on top. Every line below sets an option to something
        other than its default, or spells a default out where it is a decision worth reading in code.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@FullCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> From appsettings</h3>
    <p>
        Every option can come from configuration instead. Bind the section the way a trimmed or AOT app binds any
        options: the configuration binder's source generator (<code>EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator</code> in the
        app's project) handles <code>BelemetryOptions</code> without reflection, and Belemetry takes no dependency on
        the binder. What only code can add - custom exporters, processors, a token provider - goes on top.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@BindCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
    <CodeSnippet Code="@AppsettingsCode" Language="JSON" />
    <p>
        Two binder rules worth knowing: a bound list adds to the option's defaults
        (<code>Privacy.RedactQueryParameters</code> keeps its built-in names and gains yours), and a bound array
        replaces nothing either, which is why <code>Metrics.HistogramBoundaries</code> is empty by default and means
        the OpenTelemetry defaults until you set it. Enums and flags bind by name
        (<code>"Features": "Errors, Logs, Traces"</code>), <code>TimeSpan</code>s by <code>hh:mm:ss</code>.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Per-category log levels</h3>
    <p>
        <code>Logs.MinimumLevel</code> is the floor: nothing below it is collected, whatever a category says.
        Above it, per-category levels use the standard logging configuration, under the <code>Belemetry</code>
        provider alias, so they can differ from what the console logger shows.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@LoggingCode" Language="JSON" Expanded="true" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Environment variables</h3>
    <p>
        Outside the browser, with <code>options.UseEnvironmentVariables</code> (on by default), the standard
        variables fill in what the code leaves unset - this is how the .NET Aspire AppHost and most container
        platforms wire telemetry in. A value set in code always wins, as in the OpenTelemetry SDKs (headers merge per
        key) - except <code>OTEL_SDK_DISABLED</code>, the operator's kill switch.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Effect</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_SDK_DISABLED</code></td><td><code>true</code> (any case) turns Belemetry off entirely, whatever the code says</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_SERVICE_NAME</code></td><td><code>service.name</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES</code></td><td>Extra resource attributes (<code>k=v,k2=v2</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code></td><td>Adds an OTLP exporter when none is configured, and fills unset endpoints</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT</code>, <code>_LOGS_ENDPOINT</code>, <code>_METRICS_ENDPOINT</code></td><td>Per-signal endpoints; any of them adds the exporter too</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS</code></td><td>OTLP headers (<code>k=v,k2=v2</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL</code></td><td><code>http/protobuf</code>, <code>http/json</code> or <code>grpc</code>, unless <code>Protocol</code> is set in code</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_COMPRESSION</code>, <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TIMEOUT</code></td><td><code>gzip</code>/<code>none</code> and the timeout in milliseconds, unless set in code</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING</code></td><td>Adds an Azure Monitor exporter when none is configured</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>WEBSITE_*</code>, <code>REGION_NAME</code>, <code>CONTAINER_APP_*</code></td><td>The Azure App Service, Functions or Container Apps resource attributes and instance id of a server</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <div class="bb-note">
        <p>
            The browser has no environment. In a Blazor Web App the browser script gets its configuration from the
            <em>server's</em> options through <code>&lt;BelemetryScript /&gt;</code>, so variables set on the server reach
            it. A standalone WebAssembly app has to set everything in code.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

